<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Image with Caption</title>
  <style>
    .img-container {
      text-align: center;
    }
    .img-container img {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: auto;
    }
    .img-container p {
      margin: 0;
      font-style: italic;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="img-container">
    <a href="http://www.example.com">
      <img src="image.jpg" alt="Image">
    </a>
    <p>Caption goes here</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have an `<img>` tag with a `src` attribute set to the path of the image file and an `alt` attribute set to a descriptive alternative text for the image. We also have a `<p>` tag with some text that will serve as the caption for the image. The `<a>` tag is used to create a hyperlink to another webpage, which will open when the user clicks on the image. The `href` attribute is set to the URL of the target webpage.

The `.img-container` class is used to style the container element that holds the image and the caption. The `text-align` property is set to `center` to align the content horizontally, and the `max-width` and `height` properties are set to `100%` and `auto`, respectively, to ensure that the image resizes correctly based on the screen size. The `font-style` property is set to `italic` to apply a subtle italic style to the caption text.

