Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and returns `false` if it's not set to `true`. If the environment is set to `true`, it calls the next function in the middleware chain.

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext) {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv !== 'true') {
      return false;
    }

    return true;
  }
}
```

To use this middleware, you can apply it to your routes or controllers like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvMiddleware } from './env.middleware';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  @Get()
  @UseMiddleware(new EnvMiddleware())
  getData() {
    // Your controller logic here
  }
}
```

This way, when the request comes in, the `EnvMiddleware` will be executed first. If the `NODE_ENV` variable is not set to `true`, the middleware will return `false`, and the request will be stopped. Otherwise, it will allow the request to proceed to the next middleware or controller.

